export const services=[
["AI Visibility Score Audit","Establish a tool-specific baseline by checking how your brand appears for relevant AI search prompts."],
["ChatGPT & Gemini Visibility","Review brand mentions, descriptions and available citations across the platforms included in your package."],
["GEO & AEO Optimization","Get practical recommendations to make key pages clearer, more accessible and easier to use as answers."],
["Authority & Trust Signals","Identify where stronger evidence, consistent business details and credible references can support your claims."],
["AI-Ready Content & Entities","Improve content structure and clarify how your business, services and expertise relate."],
["Actionable Improvement Strategy","Receive prioritized next steps based on the pages, platforms and prompts reviewed."]
];
export const articles=[
["what-is-generative-engine-optimization","What Is Generative Engine Optimization (GEO)?","GEO fundamentals","GEO is the practice of improving how clearly a brand, its expertise and its content can be understood and surfaced in AI-generated answers.",[["How is GEO different from SEO?","SEO improves a website’s ability to be discovered and perform in search results. GEO considers how a brand and its information may be retrieved, interpreted, summarized or cited in generative search experiences. The disciplines overlap: useful content, accessible pages and clear entities can support both."],["What does GEO involve?","A practical program can include technical accessibility checks, clear organization and service information, answer-oriented content, consistent entity details, credible references and structured data where appropriate. No single tactic guarantees that an AI system will mention a business."],["Who should consider GEO?","Businesses whose customers research options through conversational search may benefit from understanding their AI-search presence. Start with buyer questions, the pages that answer them and evidence supporting the business’s claims."],["How is GEO measured?","Track a documented set of prompts, brand mentions, citations, cited URLs and answer context across selected platforms and dates. Results vary, so these observations are directional evidence—not a universal visibility score."]]],
["improve-website-visibility-in-chatgpt","How to Improve Website Visibility in ChatGPT","Practical guides","Make your site more useful to ChatGPT search by publishing clear, accessible information. Search inclusion is not guaranteed, and crawler access is only one part of discoverability.",[["Start with customer questions","List specific questions about services, process, pricing factors, location, expertise and comparisons. Create pages that answer them directly, then add the context readers need."],["Make your business easy to understand","Use consistent business names, descriptions, contact details and service terminology. Explain the relationships between your organization, people, products and services."],["Check search crawler access","OpenAI identifies OAI-SearchBot as its crawler for surfacing websites in ChatGPT search. GPTBot is a separate user-agent used for potential model training. Check your robots.txt, CDN and hosting rules for the user-agent relevant to your goals, and make a separate decision about training access."],["Improve usefulness and accessibility","Keep important information in readable page text, use descriptive headings, link related pages and avoid hiding essential facts inside images. Check that public pages load reliably."],["Earn trust through evidence","Support claims with named authors where appropriate, original examples, transparent methods and credible references. Never invent credentials, reviews or statistics."],["Measure without guarantees","Test a stable prompt set over time, recording date, platform, wording, mentions and citations. Responses vary; no website change guarantees inclusion."]]],
["geo-vs-seo","GEO vs SEO: What Is the Difference?","Comparisons","SEO improves visibility in search engines; GEO focuses on how a brand and its information may be represented in generative search answers. They are complementary disciplines.",[["What SEO covers","SEO includes technical accessibility, indexability, information architecture, content relevance, links and user experience. Common measures include impressions, rankings, clicks and conversions."],["What GEO covers","GEO examines whether AI systems can understand a brand’s identity, expertise and content, and whether they surface or cite that information in generated responses."],["Where they overlap","Both benefit from useful original content, clear page structure, reliable technical foundations and credible evidence. GEO adds a lens focused on generated answers and entity understanding."],["Should you choose one?","Maintain sound SEO while testing whether GEO work addresses a real audience need. Avoid abandoning established search practices for speculative ranking promises."]]],
["what-is-ai-visibility","What Is AI Visibility and How Is It Measured?","Measurement","AI visibility describes how often and in what context a brand appears in answers produced by selected AI search systems for relevant questions. It is not one universal score.",[["What should be measured?","Useful observations include brand mentions, linked citations, cited pages, answer context, competitor mentions and whether the answer accurately describes the business."],["Build a repeatable prompt set","Choose questions based on real customer journeys: category discovery, problem solving, comparisons and provider selection. Document wording and platform list."],["Record the conditions","Save the date, platform, prompt, answer and source links. Results may vary by location, account, model version and time."],["Avoid misleading scores","A percentage based on a small sample is not a market-wide rating. Explain the sample and calculation, and never imply guaranteed performance."]]],
["how-chatgpt-finds-and-cites-websites","How Does ChatGPT Find and Cite Websites?","AI search","When ChatGPT search is used, it may retrieve information from web sources and provide links to supporting pages. The retrieval and citation process can vary by product mode and system behaviour.",[["Retrieval and model knowledge","Some responses draw on learned model knowledge; search-enabled experiences can also retrieve current web information. A citation is a link associated with an answer, not proof every statement on a page was used."],["Why might a page be useful?","A page is more useful when it directly answers a question, explains claims, provides context and is accessible. Accurate authorship, dates and references help readers assess reliability."],["Can a website force a citation?","No. Website owners cannot guarantee that ChatGPT will retrieve, quote or cite a page. They can improve quality and accessibility, then observe outcomes through careful testing."],["How should businesses prepare?","Publish useful pages for real questions, keep important facts current, make identity clear and monitor citations using a consistent prompt set."]]],
["what-is-answer-engine-optimization","What Is Answer Engine Optimization (AEO)?","GEO fundamentals","AEO is the practice of structuring useful information so answer-focused systems and people can find and understand direct responses to questions.",[["What makes content answer-friendly?","Start with a clear response, then explain reasoning, steps, examples and exceptions. Use headings that describe the question and keep sections focused."],["AEO and direct answers","AEO has roots in optimizing for direct-answer experiences such as featured snippets and voice assistants. The term is also used for conversational and AI-powered answer interfaces."],["How does AEO relate to GEO?","AEO emphasizes answering questions clearly. GEO considers broader brand representation and discoverability in generative systems. Practices overlap, but the terms are not exact synonyms."],["A simple AEO workflow","Research audience questions, map each to the best page, write a direct answer, add helpful context, link supporting resources and review accuracy."]]],
["how-to-perform-a-geo-audit","How to Perform a GEO Audit","Practical guides","A GEO audit reviews whether a website communicates a clear, credible and accessible picture of a business for AI-powered discovery and answer experiences.",[["1. Define the scope","Choose the business, audience, services, competitors and AI platforms. Agree what the audit can and cannot measure before collecting evidence."],["2. Review technical accessibility","Inspect important URLs, rendering, internal links, page status and whether key content is available as text. Note access restrictions."],["3. Review entities and claims","Check that the organization, people, services, locations and contact information are consistent. Flag unsupported claims and missing context."],["4. Assess content quality","Review whether key pages answer real questions, explain processes and limitations, demonstrate expertise and link to useful material."],["5. Run documented prompt tests","Use relevant prompts and record mentions, citations, competitors and inaccuracies with dates and platform context."],["6. Prioritize actions","Separate technical issues from content improvements and longer-term authority work. Deliver evidence and practical next steps, not a fabricated score."]]],
["optimize-website-content-for-ai-search","How to Optimize Website Content for AI Search","Content strategy","Content for AI search should be written for people first: answer a real question accurately, provide enough context and make important claims clear.",[["Lead with the answer","Give readers a concise response near the beginning. Follow it with definitions, examples, steps and caveats rather than repeating keywords."],["Use meaningful structure","Organize content with descriptive headings, focused sections, lists where useful and links to deeper explanations."],["Show evidence and expertise","Identify authors where appropriate, explain methods, cite credible sources and distinguish firsthand experience from general explanation."],["Connect related concepts","Link service pages, guides, company information and relevant case studies so readers understand how topics relate."],["Review for usefulness","Ask whether the page answers the question, supports its claims and helps a reader decide what to do next."]]],
["what-is-entity-seo","What Is Entity SEO?","SEO foundations","Entity SEO helps search systems understand distinct things—such as a business, person, product, place or service—and the relationships between them.",[["What is an entity?","An entity is a distinct identifiable subject. For a consultancy, relevant entities may include the business, founder, services, location and publications."],["Why consistency matters","Different names, descriptions or contact details across important sources can make it harder to connect references confidently. Accurate consistency reduces ambiguity."],["How to strengthen entity clarity","Create a useful About page, explain services precisely, identify authors, maintain accurate profiles and connect related pages with descriptive links. Use structured data when it reflects visible content."],["What entity SEO cannot do","Adding schema or repeating a name does not guarantee a knowledge panel, ranking or AI recommendation. Entity clarity is one part of broader discoverability."]]],
["measure-ai-search-visibility","How to Measure AI Search Visibility","Measurement","Measure AI search visibility with documented prompts, platforms and observations. Track mentions and citations over time while acknowledging generated answers can change.",[["Choose meaningful questions","Include category, problem, comparison and provider-selection questions that reflect customer research. Avoid prompts designed only to produce favourable mentions."],["Set a consistent method","Document platform, mode, location context where relevant, exact prompt and date. Repeat the same tests periodically."],["Track more than mentions","Record brand appearance, cited URLs, description accuracy, answer context and competitor presence."],["Report limitations honestly","A small sample is not representative of every user or query. Do not compare different methods as if equivalent or promise a specific score."],["Turn observations into action","Improve unclear pages, correct inconsistent facts and create content that fills genuine information gaps. Re-test with the same method."]]]
,
["ai-search-for-small-and-local-businesses","AI Search for Small and Local Businesses","Small business GEO","Practical answers for smaller organizations exploring AI search without losing sight of local visibility, useful service pages and realistic budgets.",[
["Can a small business benefit from GEO?","A small business can benefit when potential customers use conversational search to compare providers, understand a service or find a local option. GEO is not a shortcut around a useful website or accurate local information. Start by making the business name, service area, contact route and actual offer easy to confirm. Add clear pages for the services customers need and answer practical questions about process, eligibility and cost. Keep relevant business listings accurate, then test a modest set of real customer questions across selected platforms. Results vary, so judge improvements by clarity, useful visits and documented mentions—not a promised placement."],
["How can I get my business listed in ChatGPT?","There is no application that guarantees a business listing or recommendation in ChatGPT answers. For ChatGPT search, OpenAI documents OAI-SearchBot as the crawler used to surface websites in search results; allowing that crawler may support discovery, but it does not guarantee a mention. Make sure your public site explains what the business does, where it operates and how customers can verify key claims. Keep authoritative profiles and contact details consistent. Then test specific customer questions and record the date, answer and any citations. A listing or mention can vary by query, search mode and time. Keep the core business facts on pages you control so visitors can confirm them independently."],
["Does GEO replace local SEO?","No. GEO does not replace local SEO or the basics that help customers find and assess a nearby business. Accurate location details, useful service-area pages, crawlable contact information and a well-maintained business profile still matter for local discovery. GEO adds a way to review how a brand and its services appear in generated answers, but there is no separate checklist that guarantees inclusion. A practical plan combines established local-search work with a few transparent AI-answer tests. If the business information is incomplete or inconsistent, correct that first rather than investing in speculative tactics. The practical objective is to make local information reliable for people and crawlable by search engines."],
["What should a local business say about its location and service area?","State the real operating location or service area plainly and distinguish a staffed customer-facing address from an area served remotely. Explain which neighborhoods or regions are covered, whether travel fees or appointment requirements apply, and how a customer can confirm availability. Keep these details consistent across the website and profiles that the business controls. Do not create near-identical pages for every neighborhood if the service and information are unchanged; that can make the site less useful. A clear location page should help a person decide whether the business can actually serve them, not just repeat place names. Review these details whenever coverage changes, so old service-area claims do not remain online."],
["Should a small business create a separate page for every service?","Create a separate page when a service has meaningfully different customers, process, requirements or evidence that deserve a complete explanation. A short page that only swaps the service name or location can add little value and create maintenance work. If several offers are closely related, one well-organized page with clear sections may serve readers better. Before publishing, ask what decision the page helps someone make and what information is unique to it. Include real scope, limitations, next steps and links to related services. Search systems do not require a fixed number of pages. Consolidating overlapping offers also makes future updates easier to maintain."],
]],
["how-ai-platforms-use-web-sources","How AI Platforms Use Web Sources","AI search platforms","A platform-by-platform guide to search grounding, citations and why the same question can produce different sources in different AI products.",[
["How does Gemini use Google Search to answer questions?","Some Gemini experiences can use Google Search to ground responses in current web information and provide source links. Google's Gemini API documentation describes a search-grounding tool that can generate queries, process results and return citations; this API behavior should not be assumed to describe every Gemini app mode or every answer. A business cannot direct Gemini to cite a particular URL. Keep important pages accessible, specific and supported by evidence, then inspect the sources shown for a documented sample of relevant questions. Product behavior and availability can change, so check the current product documentation when making technical decisions. For a site owner, the actionable point is to make the source material accurate and easy to check."],
["How does Perplexity provide sources in an answer?","Perplexity describes its PerplexityBot as a crawler used to surface and link websites in its search results. In an individual answer, the product may display linked sources alongside generated text, giving readers a route to inspect the material. The presence of a source link does not prove that every sentence on a page supports every claim in the answer, so users should open the cited page and check context. Website owners can review crawler access and server or security logs, but allowing the bot does not guarantee a citation. Results can differ by prompt, available sources and time. Keep each page focused on what it can verify, rather than trying to influence citation selection."],
["How does Claude web search cite sources?","Anthropic's help documentation says that when Claude web search is used, it can search multiple sources and include citations and links in its response. Web search may be enabled or invoked differently across accounts and product experiences, so a website owner should not assume that every Claude conversation uses live web retrieval. Citations are useful for checking the answer, but they are not endorsements and may not support every interpretation. Publish accurate, accessible source pages and make important facts easy to verify. If you test Claude, record whether web search was active and save the exact prompt and cited URLs."],
["Do different AI search platforms use the same sources?","Not necessarily. Each product can use different search tools, indexes, retrieval steps, ranking systems and citation interfaces. Even when two platforms can access the same public page, they may choose different sources for the same question or show different links on another day. A website appearing in one answer is therefore not evidence that it will appear in another product. Compare platforms with the same clearly worded prompts, dates and locations where relevant, but report each platform separately. Avoid combining a small number of observations into a universal visibility claim; the sample only describes the tests you actually ran. This is why cross-platform reports should preserve examples instead of collapsing them into one number."],
["Can a business tell which AI platform visited its website?","Sometimes. Server or CDN logs may record recognizable crawler user agents, while analytics may show a referring domain when a person clicks through from an AI answer. These signals describe different events: an automated crawler request is not a human visit, and a referral does not reveal every answer that mentioned the brand. User agents can also be imitated, so they are not sufficient proof of a bot's identity. Use platform-published verification guidance or IP information where available, and review analytics privacy settings. Treat logs and referrals as partial evidence, not a complete record of AI visibility. Review both log evidence and clicked sessions before drawing conclusions about reach."],
]],
["creating-useful-pages-for-ai-search","Creating Useful Pages for AI Search","Content strategy","Answer customer needs with clear pages, grounded details and useful context instead of chasing a supposed formula for AI citations.",[
["Does longer content perform better in AI search?","There is no dependable word-count target that guarantees a citation or a stronger answer. A useful page may be short when the question is simple, or longer when readers need steps, comparisons, examples and limitations. Google's helpful-content guidance explicitly advises against writing to a word count because someone claims a search engine prefers it. Cover the decision the reader is trying to make, remove repeated material and support important claims. If a page feels incomplete, add the missing evidence or explanation; if it is padded, edit it down. Measure usefulness, not length alone. A shorter page that resolves the question well can be more complete for that reader."],
["Does every customer question need its own webpage?","No. Give a question its own page only when it deserves a substantial, distinct answer that helps someone complete a task or make a decision. Related questions can sit together when they share the same intent and context, with descriptive headings that make each answer easy to find. Separate pages are more appropriate when the audience, service, evidence or next step differs. Avoid publishing dozens of thin pages that repeat the same advice with small wording changes. First map questions to existing content, then fill genuine gaps. Clear internal links help readers move from a short answer to the fuller explanation."],
["How should a business publish prices that depend on the project?","Explain what determines the price instead of presenting a misleading fixed amount. Describe the main variables—such as scope, location, materials, usage or support level—then provide a real starting range only if the business can substantiate and maintain it. Say what is included, what may cost extra and how to get a tailored quote. Keep the same facts consistent on the website and sales materials, and label temporary offers with their conditions and end dates. A direct, qualified explanation is more useful than hiding all pricing behind a form or implying that every customer pays the same amount. This gives prospects a realistic basis for comparison before they contact the business."],
["How can case studies support an AI-search-friendly website?","A case study can help readers understand how a service works when it documents a real project with permission, context and verifiable details. Explain the client's starting situation, the work performed, the timeframe and the outcome that can be evidenced; distinguish measured results from observations or client feedback. State relevant limitations, such as seasonality or other changes that may have influenced the result. Do not invent clients, percentages or testimonials, and do not imply that one outcome is typical. A well-sourced case study gives people something concrete to evaluate; no format guarantees that an AI system will quote it. Permission to share client information should be documented before publishing names or results."],
["How can original research make a business article more useful?","Original research adds value when the business has a sound method and can explain what the findings do—and do not—show. Define the question, sample, collection period and analysis before drawing conclusions. Publish enough methodology for another reader to understand the limits, protect personal information and avoid presenting a small or biased sample as representative of an entire market. If the business does not have evidence, it is better to explain established sources and identify uncertainty than to manufacture a survey or statistic. Useful first-hand data can differentiate a page for readers, but it does not guarantee rankings, citations or recommendations."],
]],
["ai-brand-mentions-citations-and-corrections","AI Brand Mentions, Citations and Corrections","Brand presence","Understand the difference between being named, being linked and being represented accurately in generated answers.",[
["What is the difference between an AI brand mention and a citation?","A brand mention is text that names or describes a business in an answer. A citation is a link to a source that the product presents as supporting or related to the response. A brand may be mentioned without a linked page, and a business page can be cited without the brand being the focus. Track these separately, along with the surrounding wording and whether the description is accurate. Neither signal alone proves that most users see the same response, that the page caused the mention or that the platform recommends the business. A report should preserve the full answer because a bare count loses this distinction."],
["Why might an AI answer cite a competitor but not my business?","A single answer cannot reveal the system's full selection logic. The competitor's page may more directly answer that particular wording, contain a detail the response needed, or simply have been retrieved for that test. Your page may be absent from the source set, unclear, inaccessible or not relevant to the specific question. Compare the pages and citations rather than assuming a hidden ranking penalty. Improve the real information gap—such as a missing service explanation or evidence—then repeat the same test. One result is an observation, not proof that the competitor is universally preferred. Test the same question again later before changing a page based on one response."],
["Why might an AI assistant describe a business incorrectly?","Generated systems can combine outdated, incomplete or conflicting information, misunderstand a page or produce an unsupported statement. First capture the exact answer, date, product mode and any cited sources. Check whether the error is already present on a page or external profile the business controls; correct the source and keep important facts consistent. If the answer cites a page, inspect whether the page itself is ambiguous. Then use the product's available feedback or reporting route when appropriate. A correction to a source may take time to appear elsewhere, and changing one page cannot guarantee that every future answer will be accurate."],
["Can a business request correction of an inaccurate AI answer?","Often there is a feedback, report or content-removal process, but the available route depends on the platform and the type of error. Save the answer and source links, identify the specific factual problem, and provide evidence from an authoritative source. If the mistake comes from the business's own page or profile, correct that source first. A report is not a guarantee of removal or immediate correction, especially if an answer combines several sources or relies on model knowledge. Keep records of what was submitted and recheck later. Do not use a correction request to suppress a valid opinion or accurate reporting."],
["Does an AI citation mean that the platform endorses a business?","No. A citation indicates that a page was linked as a source or related resource in a particular response; it is not an endorsement, certification or promise that the product recommends the business. The answer may include several sources with different viewpoints, and a link may support only part of the text. Read the cited material in context and check its date and authority before relying on it. Businesses should avoid describing a citation as a formal approval or client result. When reporting visibility, show the original prompt, answer and link so readers can judge what the citation actually means."],
]],
["structured-data-and-entities-for-ai-search","Structured Data and Business Entities for AI Search","Technical foundations","Understand how structured information can clarify a website without mistaking markup for a shortcut to AI visibility.",[
["Can structured data help search systems understand a business?","Structured data gives search systems explicit labels about information on a page, such as an organization, product or article. It can reduce ambiguity when it accurately describes content that visitors can also see. Google explains that structured data can help it understand page content and may make a page eligible for supported search features, but eligibility is not a guarantee of display. Other AI products may use different retrieval and interpretation systems. Add markup only when it is correct, maintained and appropriate to the visible page; it complements clear content rather than replacing it. Markup is strongest when it reinforces a page that already explains the subject clearly."],
["Does a business need special schema to appear in AI Overviews?","Google says there are no additional technical requirements or special structured-data types needed for AI Overviews or AI Mode. Pages still need to meet ordinary Search eligibility requirements, including being indexed and eligible to appear with a snippet, and inclusion is never guaranteed. Structured data should describe visible, accurate information and can support general understanding or other eligible rich results, but adding more markup is not an AI Overview switch. Prioritize crawlable pages, clear useful content and consistency between markup and what visitors actually see. For most sites, access and readable content are more immediate checks than new markup alone."],
["What is llms.txt, and does a website need one?","llms.txt is a proposed text-file convention intended to give language-model tools a curated guide to a website. It is not a universal technical requirement for Google AI Overviews, and Google says site owners do not need to create special AI files to appear in its AI features. Other products may choose to support conventions differently, so do not treat the file as a ranking or citation mechanism. A clear sitemap, useful internal links, accessible page text and accurate robots rules remain more established parts of website management. Add optional files only for a defined use case and maintain their contents."],
["What should Organization structured data include?","Organization markup should reflect facts that are genuinely visible and verifiable on the website, such as the organization name, official URL, logo and appropriate contact details. Only include properties that apply and can be kept accurate; do not invent awards, locations, staff, reviews or service claims to fill optional fields. Connect official profiles only when they clearly represent the same organization. Validate the markup and compare it with the page a visitor sees. Structured data can clarify meaning, but it does not guarantee a knowledge panel, a rich result, a citation or a recommendation in an AI answer. If a detail cannot be confirmed, leave it out instead of guessing."],
["When should a business use sameAs links?","The sameAs property can connect a structured-data entity to reliable pages that identify the same person or organization, such as an official social profile or a recognized business directory listing. Use only profiles the business controls or can confidently verify; a loosely related page can create more ambiguity than clarity. Keep the visible name and identity consistent and ensure the linked profile is current. sameAs is a descriptive clue, not proof that a search engine will merge every reference or show a knowledge panel. It cannot compensate for missing information or inconsistent claims elsewhere. Review these links periodically so closed or reassigned accounts do not keep representing the organization."],
]],
["ai-search-crawlers-and-website-controls","AI Search Crawlers and Website Controls","Technical foundations","A practical guide to crawler names, robots.txt choices and the limits of controlling automated access.",[
["Which OpenAI crawler is used for ChatGPT search results?","OpenAI identifies OAI-SearchBot as the crawler used to surface websites in ChatGPT search features. A site owner who wants pages considered for those search results can review whether robots.txt, hosting rules or a web application firewall blocks that user agent. Allowing it only makes crawling possible; it does not promise that a page will be retrieved, cited or mentioned. Check the current bot documentation and server logs before changing access rules. Make the decision deliberately, since crawler access and model-training preferences are separate controls. Review the user-agent list in robots.txt and ask your host whether its firewall applies separate bot rules."],
["Is OAI-SearchBot the same as GPTBot?","No. OpenAI documents OAI-SearchBot for surfacing sites in ChatGPT search results and GPTBot for crawling content that may be used to improve foundation models. Their robots.txt settings are independent. A publisher can choose to permit search crawling while disallowing GPTBot, or make another choice based on its goals and legal advice. Review the current documentation, use the exact user-agent tokens and check for conflicting rules in robots.txt or infrastructure. These controls affect the relevant automated crawls; they do not let a site owner dictate how every user-requested fetch or previously learned fact is handled. Revisit the choice if the site content licensing or business policy changes."],
["What does Google-Extended control, and does it block Google Search?","Google documents Google-Extended as a crawler token that publishers can use to manage access for certain other Google systems, including some generative-AI training or grounding uses. Google Search crawling and AI Search features are governed by Googlebot and the normal Search controls; blocking Google-Extended is not the same as blocking a page from Search or opting it out of AI Overviews. Check Google's current common-crawler documentation before editing robots.txt, because the exact products affected can change. If the goal is to limit Search snippets, use the Search preview controls designed for that purpose. Separate the Search goal from the distinct product controls in any policy decision."],
["What is PerplexityBot used for?","Perplexity says PerplexityBot is used to surface and link websites in its search results, and states that it is not used to crawl content for AI foundation-model training. Perplexity-User is a separate user-triggered fetcher that may visit a page to help answer a person's question. Site owners can make their own access decisions through robots.txt and infrastructure settings, then verify behavior against Perplexity's current crawler documentation and published IP information. Allowing the crawler does not guarantee a citation, and a user-triggered request is different from automatic indexing. Monitor server responses after policy changes to confirm the intended crawler can reach public pages."],
["Can robots.txt guarantee that nobody can access or use a webpage?","No. robots.txt is a request to compliant crawlers, not an access-control system or a legal guarantee that information will remain private. It does not replace authentication, server permissions or a removal process, and different products may use different crawlers or user-triggered fetches. To protect private information, keep it behind proper access controls instead of relying on a robots rule. For public pages, use the specific platform controls that match the goal, check CDN and WAF behavior, and understand that previously indexed or independently copied material may not disappear immediately after a crawl rule changes. Use authentication for non-public material, not a crawler exclusion rule."],
]],
["google-ai-overviews-for-website-owners","Google AI Overviews: What Website Owners Need to Know","Google Search","What Google's current published guidance says about supporting links, eligibility and controls for AI Overviews.",[
["Does a page need to be indexed to appear as a supporting link in an AI Overview?","Google says a page must be indexed and eligible to appear in Google Search with a snippet to be eligible as a supporting link in AI Overviews or AI Mode. Meeting those technical requirements does not guarantee that Google will crawl, index or show the page. A site owner should first check normal Search accessibility, indexing and snippet eligibility, then focus on useful information that answers the visitor's need. There is no separate submission form that guarantees an AI Overview link. Use Search Console and URL inspection to diagnose a specific page rather than infer status from one search. Eligibility is only a prerequisite, not a signal that a page will be selected."],
["Does Google require special AI Overview optimization or markup?","No special schema, AI text file or separate AI Overview optimization is required according to Google's published guidance. Google says its existing SEO fundamentals remain relevant: make pages accessible, provide helpful people-first content, link important pages internally and ensure structured data matches the visible content. A page may be eligible without being selected, and selection can vary by query. Avoid vendors who promise that a proprietary tag or file will force an inclusion. Technical fixes should address a real crawl or indexing issue; content work should make a page more useful to people, not merely add AI-focused keywords. A good technical fix should be tied to a specific documented issue."],
["Do AI Overviews appear for every Google search?","No. Google says AI Overviews are shown when its systems determine that a generative summary can be especially helpful, so they do not appear on every query. A website may therefore have relevant, indexed content without an Overview appearing for a particular search. This makes a single manual search a poor way to estimate broad visibility. When studying a topic, record the exact query, date, location and result format, and repeat the check consistently. The absence of an Overview on one search does not show that the site has a technical problem or that AI search is irrelevant to its audience."],
["Can a website limit how much of its content appears in AI Overviews?","Google documents search-preview controls such as nosnippet, data-nosnippet and max-snippet for managing what can appear in Search previews. These controls have broader Search effects and should be selected with care; they are not a precise switch for a single AI Overview sentence. Google also notes that changes can take time to be recrawled and processed. Before applying a restriction, identify the exact content and outcome you want, read the current documentation and test the implementation with Search Console tools. Limiting snippets can also reduce the information or links available in other Search experiences. Test changes before rollout, since snippet restrictions can affect ordinary Search results too."],
["How can Google AI Overviews show links from more than one page?","Google says AI Overviews and AI Mode may use a query fan-out approach, where systems issue multiple related searches across subtopics or data sources while developing a response. This can surface supporting links from pages that address different parts of a complex question. The process does not mean every page needs to target many keywords; a focused page that explains one useful topic clearly may serve a subtopic well. Build coherent internal links between related resources and ensure each page has a distinct purpose. Google's description explains a possible system behavior, not a formula for earning a link. Each page still needs a clear topic and audience."],
]],
["measuring-ai-search-visits-and-citations","Measuring AI Search Visits and Citations","Measurement","Separate observed citations, platform referrals and conventional search data, and report the limits of each measure.",[
["Can analytics identify every visit that came from an AI answer?","No. Analytics may record a referring domain when a person clicks a link from an AI product, but referral details can be missing, grouped or lost as a visit moves between apps and browsers. Direct visits do not reveal whether someone first saw the business in an AI response. Automated crawler requests are also not human visits and should be analyzed separately in server logs. Use available referrer data as a partial signal, document your analytics setup and avoid claiming that it captures every AI-assisted journey. Pair referral observations with qualitative tests and business outcomes rather than treating one report as complete attribution."],
["How should a business calculate an AI citation rate?","Define the denominator before calculating anything. For example, a team might count the proportion of a fixed set of recorded prompt tests in which at least one source link pointed to its domain. Specify the platforms, prompt wording, dates, locations, answer modes and what qualifies as a citation. Keep brand mentions separate from domain citations, and show the sample size beside any percentage. The resulting figure describes only that test set; it is not an industry-wide visibility score or a prediction of future performance. Save examples so someone can review how the number was produced. Recalculate it only after confirming the method has not changed."],
["How many prompts are enough to measure AI visibility?","There is no universally accepted number that makes a prompt set representative of all AI-search users. Start with a manageable set derived from real customer tasks, such as learning a category, comparing approaches and checking provider suitability. Include questions where the business is relevant and questions where it should not be mentioned; otherwise the test can be biased toward favourable results. Keep the exact wording and explain why prompts were selected. If you expand the set, mark the change because results from different samples are not directly comparable. A transparent small test is more informative than a large unexplained score."],
["Does Google Search Console report AI Overview performance separately?","Google's published AI-features guidance says sites appearing in AI features are included in overall Search Console traffic, within the Web search type. That is useful for tracking broad Search performance, but it is not a universal report of every AI mention, citation or assistant referral. Reporting interfaces can change, so check the current Search Console documentation before making a decision. For a careful review, combine Search Console trends with analytics referrals, server logs where appropriate and a documented set of manual answer checks. Keep each data source separate and state what it can and cannot measure. Do not label aggregate traffic as AI Overview visits unless the available data supports that attribution."],
["How often should a business repeat AI-search tests?","Repeat tests often enough to notice meaningful changes without turning variable answers into noise. A business might recheck after a substantial site update, a major service change or a new product release, then schedule periodic reviews suited to its decision cycle. There is no official interval that guarantees a reliable measure. Use the same prompts and platform settings where possible, note when models or interfaces change, and distinguish a genuine content update from a different answer generated on a different day. If the answer changes once, record it; do not assume the fluctuation represents a lasting trend. Choose a cadence that fits the business, not an arbitrary industry benchmark."],
]],
["ai-search-for-ecommerce-and-saas","AI Search for Ecommerce and SaaS Websites","Industry guides","Practical guidance for making product facts and software use cases clear without implying that any platform will recommend a particular offer.",[
["What product facts should an ecommerce page make easy to verify?","A product page should state the actual product name, model or variant, core specifications, compatibility, current price where appropriate, availability, shipping and return conditions. Explain which details vary by size, region or seller, and keep the visible page consistent with any product feed or structured data. Use original descriptions rather than copying a manufacturer's catalogue text verbatim. Clear facts help a shopper compare options and can reduce ambiguity for systems that retrieve page information. They do not guarantee that an AI assistant will surface the product, and rapidly changing details need a reliable update process. Buyers should be able to verify these details without relying on an AI summary."],
["Can a product feed replace the information on the product page?","No. A feed can provide structured product information to the services that support it, but it does not replace a clear, accessible product page for customers. The page should explain what the item is, who it is for, its important limitations and how to buy or return it. Keep prices, variants and availability consistent between the page and any connected feed; discrepancies can confuse shoppers and make the information less trustworthy. Use each feed according to its platform's current requirements. Do not assume one product feed is read by every AI search tool or guarantees a citation. Treat the feed as one distribution channel, not the canonical customer experience."],
["How should a business explain changing prices or availability?","Show the conditions that make a price or stock status valid, such as a selected variant, region, seller or update time. If a price changes frequently, connect the displayed value to a dependable inventory or commerce system and avoid publishing stale figures in evergreen copy. Explain whether taxes, delivery or optional features are included where that affects the customer's decision. Keep structured data and feeds aligned with the visible page when used. AI answers can quote outdated pages, so no markup can guarantee instant updates elsewhere. Make it easy for a shopper to confirm the current offer before purchase. A dated review process helps catch stale facts before customers encounter them."],
["How can a SaaS website explain a product use case clearly?","Describe the user, the task they are trying to complete, how the product fits into that workflow and what conditions or plan limits apply. Concrete examples—such as the inputs needed, steps taken and output produced—are more useful than broad claims like “streamline everything.” Identify supported integrations and security or deployment details only when they are verified. Add a comparison or use-case page when it answers a distinct buyer question, and keep it current as the product changes. Clear explanations help potential customers assess fit; they do not guarantee an AI system will rank or recommend the software. Use screenshots or workflow examples only when they reflect the current product."],
["Are online reviews enough to get an ecommerce brand recommended by AI?","No. Reviews can help shoppers assess real experiences, but they are not a guaranteed recommendation signal and should not be treated as a substitute for accurate product information. Make review sources and dates clear, follow the platform's rules, and never fabricate or selectively misrepresent feedback. A useful product page should still explain specifications, compatibility, availability, returns and limitations. Generated answers may summarize third-party material, omit a product or quote information that is no longer current. Monitor how the product is represented, but do not claim that a particular review count or rating will produce an AI mention. Keep incentives and moderation transparent, and follow the review platform rules."],
]],
["responsible-geo-and-content-quality","Responsible GEO and Content Quality","Editorial standards","A practical standard for using AI tools, maintaining trustworthy pages and evaluating claims made by GEO providers.",[
["Is AI-generated content against Google Search policies?","Google's published guidance focuses on the purpose and quality of content, not simply whether an AI tool helped create it. Content produced primarily to manipulate rankings or mass-published without adding value can violate spam policies; using automation to help people with accurate, useful information is a different case. A business should review every page for factual accuracy, originality, completeness and genuine audience value. Keep accountable human oversight, especially for legal, financial, health or safety topics. Do not publish large batches merely to reach a question count, and do not assume that AI-written text is automatically compliant or automatically penalized. The editorial standard should be the same whether a draft began with AI or a person."],
["Can a business publish an AI-drafted article without human review?","It should not publish important claims without a knowledgeable person checking them. AI systems can invent sources, misstate product details, omit limitations or combine facts from different contexts. Verify names, numbers, dates, quotations and links against primary sources, then check whether the article gives useful context rather than repeating generic material. For sensitive subjects, involve an appropriately qualified reviewer and follow relevant professional rules. Keep a record of what was checked and who is responsible for corrections. A review does not guarantee a ranking, but it reduces avoidable errors and helps protect the trust of readers. Assign a named owner to approve important claims before publication."],
["Should a business disclose that AI helped write its content?","There is no single disclosure rule that applies to every website and every type of AI assistance. Requirements can depend on the subject, jurisdiction, platform policy and whether readers could be materially misled about authorship or evidence. Be transparent when disclosure is required, when AI-generated media could be mistaken for real events, or when the method is relevant to a claim. Regardless of labels, a business remains responsible for accuracy, permissions and substantiation. Check the rules that govern your industry and distribution channels, and do not imply that a human expert personally performed research or testing that did not occur."],
["When should a business update an older article?","Update an article when a meaningful fact, process, product, source or recommendation has changed, or when reader feedback reveals a real gap. Check the claims and links, revise the affected sections, and note the update date when it helps readers understand freshness. Do not change dates merely to make an unchanged page look new. Google's helpful-content guidance warns against using freshness as a superficial signal. If the page is no longer useful, consider consolidating it with a stronger resource or clearly explaining that it is historical. Keep an internal record of substantive edits so future reviews can focus on what changed."],
["What should a GEO provider promise in a visibility report?","A responsible provider can promise a defined scope, documented methods, evidence from the agreed prompts and clear recommendations; they should not guarantee rankings, citations, brand mentions or recommendations controlled by third-party platforms. Ask for the tested platforms, exact prompts, dates, sample size, screenshots or source links, and an explanation of how any score was calculated. Check that the report separates observed results from hypotheses and does not present a small sample as universal performance. Before paying, confirm what work is included and what counts as completion. A useful report makes its limits clear instead of using certainty to sell an outcome."],
]],
];
